for years why I started with a  screenplay that I wrote called drum line  and the director charles stone his  vision for the movie was so close to the  script that I wrote that a lot of  screenwriters told me you'll you'll  never have an experience like that where  exactly what you intended on the page is  so close to what the director's vision  of it was and as I went along working as  a screenwriter in the industry I began  to see that that was such a special  experience I wrote on the set I worked  with the actors and drum line and so  eventually I learned it is the  director's vision and his medium or her  medium in this case and and slowly but  surely that desire in me to direct began  and I didn't want to have that gap  between what I was writing on the page  and what the vision was of the director  and the details of the script became  more and more important to me as I wrote  and when I got to people's because it's  a comedy and african-american families  sometimes are not given the opportunity  to sort of have this kind of dry wit  sometimes quirky if I didn't protect  that tone as the director I was really  worried about giving a comedy to the  world that I didn't have that kind of  tone so that's what kind of pushed me  and then my producer Stephanie Elaine we  had a very really early on I had met  with her when I did not want to I didn't  really think I should direct this  because it was a big cast singing  dancing dogs like everything that would  make me say do not do this but in the  conversation that I first had with her  she felt that it was mine to direct and  she kind of encouraged me and championed  me to do that because there's so few  African American films it with drum line  with atl I noticed in a lot of my work I  try to hit many generations because I  sort of think about that I want mom to  go dad to go teenagers to go and  so I try to cover sort of a broad sense  of comedy and hit moments that everybody  in the family might enjoy and with the  cast I started with Kerry Washington and  she was the first person that I thought  of for people's even though she doesn't  have a comedic background she sort of  has this type a perfectionism about her  like Kerry does I would just watch her  and you just sent her know she's got it  together and she's very studied and  prepared and I felt like if she would  play with us and sort of make fun of  that kind of perfectionism that it would  be funny as the straight man with Craig  and I wanted to sell that she really  came from a striving family and a family  where high achievement was important and  even over the comedy I knew Kerry could  sell that and then she just ended up  being totally funny in the movie so it  was a bonus and and in the case of  diahann carroll and melvin van peebles  bay it was day one that we were shooting  there there's their scenes and so I had  a meltdown but it didn't occur to me  until I was going to set that okay this  is melvin van peebles diahann carroll  and then it started snowing and I and  then the ad is like well we have to  block it now inside and so I just  remember having just this quiet meltdown  that day and Diahann Carroll unknowing  that I was needed a moment just gave a  speech to the cast because she had  followed them as fans and noticed their  choices were really smart and was  excited to come play with them and  playing she doesn't do much comedy but  she doesn't do comedy she rarely works  and by choice she's very selective so to  ask her to play sort of a frisky modern  as she called it grandmother made my  heart like have palpitations but she  made this speech on the first day and  Melvin as well and it set the tone for  the movie and it kind of gave me a  minute to come back inside my body and  we had a great shoot so I thank them for  that I did have a theory with casting I  would pick African Americans that might  be third row from the back and a cool  Apatow movie  he might be like he had that line he was  so funny but um um many times Craig this  is his first leading role and and  carries for comedy like I just sort of  you know twisted everybody and how you  expected to see them kali hawk also she  had funny lines in bridesmaids like a in  and out of bridesmaids and that's where  I saw her and I was like oh yeah kali  hawk will be good at gala or um malcolm  barrett he's a new stand-up comic that  played Craig Robinson's brother in the  movie and kimrie she played Meg the  girlfriend of Gloria people's these this  was a big opportunity for us especially  as an african-american cast to break new  faces to break new leading men it's it's  something that's a struggle in our  system and so that's one of my proudest  achievements as a new director to bring  new voices to the screen that we don't  we don't get as many chances so I'm I  think I'm most proud of that
 

